consiSt("wv",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Appeals of West Virginia","d":"Court of last resort and the State’s rulemaking court. It reviews Intermediate Court of Appeals decisions by discretionary petition (W. Va. Code § 51-11-10; RAP 5), and may take any civil case filed in the Intermediate Court on its own motion or grant a party’s motion for direct review, filed within 20 days of the notice of appeal, when the appeal involves a question of fundamental public importance and time is of the essence (§§ 51-11-4(b)(1), 51-11-5; RAP 29(f)). It keeps direct appellate jurisdiction over matters the Intermediate Court cannot hear, including criminal, juvenile, abuse and neglect, Public Service Commission and lawyer-discipline cases, and it alone hears original-jurisdiction writs (mandamus, prohibition, habeas corpus, certiorari) and certified questions (RAP 1(b), 16, 17). The Clerk of the Supreme Court is also the Clerk of the Intermediate Court, and e-filing is mandatory in both (RAP 38A(c))."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Intermediate Court of Appeals of West Virginia","d":"Created by the Legislature in 2021 and hearing appeals since July 1, 2022 (W. Va. Code § 51-11-1 et seq.). It has no original jurisdiction. It hears appeals of right from final circuit court judgments in civil cases entered after June 30, 2022, final family court orders (except domestic violence), guardianship and conservatorship orders, agency and ALJ decisions, and Workers’ Compensation Board of Review decisions (§ 51-11-4(b)). It has no jurisdiction over criminal cases, juvenile and abuse-and-neglect cases, interlocutory appeals, certified questions, extraordinary writs, or Public Service Commission decisions (§ 51-11-4(d); RAP 1(b))."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Circuit Courts","d":"General-jurisdiction courts of record, organized in judicial circuits whose terms of court are fixed by Trial Court Rule 2. Civil practice is governed by the revised Rules of Civil Procedure (effective January 1, 2025) and the statewide Trial Court Rules, which preempt differing local rules (TCR 1.01–1.03). Specialized assignment bodies include the Business Court Division (seven regional assignment areas; referral by motion filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court) and the Mass Litigation Panel (TCR 26, 29). Mandatory e-filing applies in “Active Counties” under TCR 15A."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Family Courts","d":"Hear divorce, custody, support and related domestic matters under the Rules of Practice and Procedure for Family Court; the Rules of Civil Procedure apply there only as those rules allow (RCP 81(b)(1)). Final orders are appealed to the Intermediate Court within 30 days and perfected within 60 days, unless both parties file a notice of intent within 14 days to appeal directly to the Supreme Court (RAP 13(b)–(c)). Domestic violence orders go first to circuit court (§ 51-11-4(b)(2))."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Magistrate Courts","d":"Limited-jurisdiction courts governed by the Rules of Civil Procedure for the Magistrate Courts and W. Va. Code ch. 50 (neither collected). On appeal to circuit court the Rules of Civil Procedure apply, but Rules 26–37 discovery is unavailable absent an order (RCP 81(a)(1)). The jurisdictional amount was not collected."}],"slapp":[{"t":"No anti-SLAPP statute; the 2025 UPEPA bill died in committee","d":"West Virginia has not enacted an anti-SLAPP law. House Bill 3205 of the 2025 Regular Session (“Uniform Public Expression Protection”) was introduced and referred to House Judiciary on March 6, 2025, and that is its last recorded action. The 2026 Regular Session bill list shows no public-expression or anti-SLAPP bill. The only related item found was SB 668 (“Providing compensation to victims of abusive lawsuits”), referred to Senate Judiciary on January 29, 2026 with no further action; its text was not reviewed. The Code’s Chapter 55 (civil remedies) has no such article; its articles end at Article 21 (commercial receiverships).\nHow this was checked: the Legislature’s 2025 and 2026 all-bills lists (searched for “public expression”, “SLAPP” and “strategic lawsuit”), the HB 3205 and SB 668 history pages, and the Chapter 55 article index on code.wvlegislature.gov. Special sessions were not searched, and the Code site’s keyword search proved unreliable.","c":"2025 Reg. Sess. HB 3205 (died in committee); 2026 Reg. Sess. SB 668 (died in committee); W. Va. Code ch. 55","v":true},{"t":"What a defendant uses instead","d":"There is no special motion, discovery stay or fee-shifting for speech-based suits. Defenses come through ordinary procedure: a Rule 12(b)(6) motion (on which the court must state findings and conclusions when it grants the motion, RCP 52(a)(3)); summary judgment, which may be filed until 30 days after discovery closes (RCP 56(b)); Rule 11 sanctions after the 21-day safe harbor; and protective orders under RCP 26(c). Denial of a motion to dismiss cannot be appealed to the Intermediate Court because it has no interlocutory jurisdiction. The routes are a certified question under W. Va. Code § 58-5-2 (which covers denied summary-judgment motions and pleading-sufficiency questions) or a writ of prohibition in the Supreme Court (RAP 16).","c":"W. Va. R. Civ. P. 11(c), 12(b), 26(c), 52(a)(3), 56(b); W. Va. Code §§ 51-11-4(d)(8), 58-5-2; RAP 16, 17","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice under Admission Rule 8.0: a West Virginia local counsel with a West Virginia office, who must attend everything","d":"A lawyer licensed in another U.S. state, territory or possession may apply to appear in a particular case before any West Virginia court, board or agency, if that jurisdiction extends like courtesy to West Virginia lawyers. The lawyer must first associate with local counsel: an active West Virginia State Bar member who maintains an actual physical office in West Virginia that is the primary place from which local counsel practices daily.\nLocal counsel’s name and West Virginia bar number must appear on every filing. Local counsel must personally appear at all pretrial conferences, hearings and trials, and must attend depositions and other out-of-court events unless the judge allows remote attendance. Local counsel shares joint responsibility for the case, and any paper that must be served is invalid unless local counsel signs it physically or electronically. A rule note citing Parnell v. Supreme Court of Appeals (4th Cir. 1997) upholds the office requirement.","c":"W. Va. R. Admission 8.0(a)–(b); TCR 4.02","v":true},{"t":"Application: verified statement, $350 per lawyer per case, $350 annual fee","d":"The out-of-state lawyer gives local counsel a verified application to file with the tribunal and the West Virginia State Bar. It must list:\n- all West Virginia matters, including temporary Rule 5.5 practice, by the applicant and by the applicant’s firm in the preceding 24 months;\n- good standing everywhere admitted, and no discipline in the preceding 24 months;\n- an agreement to comply with West Virginia law, including taxes and pro bono/indigent-defense standards;\n- local counsel’s endorsement.\nA non-refundable $350 fee is due to the State Bar for each applicant in each case. The motion must include a verified statement that the fee was paid and, under TCR 4.02, that notice was sent to the State Bar. Each civil action number is a separate case, so suits split under RCP 3(a) need separate admissions. A lawyer admitted for one year or more also owes an annual $350 fee per lawyer, and admission ends if that fee is not paid within 30 days of the due date. The tribunal may grant temporary admission for good cause with a deadline to complete the application, and may deny admission if the applicant’s West Virginia appearances in the past 24 months are “numerous or frequent”. The rule sets no fixed numerical cap.","c":"W. Va. R. Admission 8.0(c)–(e); TCR 4.02; W. Va. R. Civ. P. 3(a)","v":true},{"t":"Admission does not follow the case to the appellate courts","d":"Pro hac vice admission carries over if venue is transferred, but it does not carry into the Supreme Court of Appeals on appeal or in an extraordinary proceeding. A new application and fee are required there. RAP 3(c) applies the same rule to each appellate tribunal, including the Intermediate Court. A filing may be lodged before admission only if a complete pro hac vice motion is filed at the same time.","c":"W. Va. R. Admission 8.0(f); RAP 3(c)","v":true},{"t":"Non-compliant filings are rejected or stricken","d":"Circuit clerks must reject any filing not signed as Rule 8.0 requires. A non-compliant document may be expunged as a fugitive document, but a party has 30 days after notice to cure by filing a certification signed by local counsel. Separately, under TCR 4.02 papers filed by a visiting attorney who has not complied may be stricken after 15 days’ written notice.","c":"W. Va. R. Admission 8.0(j); TCR 4.02","v":true},{"t":"Work allowed without a pro hac vice order, and the Rule 5.5(f) disclosure","d":"Subject to RPC 5.5, an out-of-state lawyer may do the following without admission:\n- work in West Virginia on a proceeding pending elsewhere in which the lawyer is admitted;\n- if the lawyer reasonably expects to qualify for pro hac vice, consult with West Virginia counsel or a prospective client and prepare a potential West Virginia case;\n- prepare for and take part in arbitration, mediation or other ADR anywhere.\nWest Virginia RPC 5.5(f), which has no ABA Model Rule counterpart, requires a lawyer working under 5.5(c) or (d) to tell the client affirmatively, before providing services, that the lawyer is not admitted in West Virginia. Pro hac vice applications must disclose all temporary Rule 5.5 practice in West Virginia.","c":"W. Va. R. Admission 8.0(k), 8.0(c)(4); W. Va. RPC 5.5(c), (f)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope representation: permitted by the ethics rules, but no court procedure for limited appearances","d":"RPC 1.2(c) lets a lawyer limit the scope of a representation with informed consent, and RPC 6.5 covers short-term programs. The collected Rules of Civil Procedure and Trial Court Rules contain no limited-appearance or notice-of-limited-scope procedure. Once a lawyer appears in a civil action, withdrawal requires a court order after notice to the client covering eight specified points and a written notification certificate (TCR 4.03(b)). Withdrawal in the appellate courts requires proof of compliance with TCR 4.03, and the lawyer stays responsible for deadlines until the order is entered (RAP 3(d)).","c":"W. Va. RPC 1.2(c), 6.5; TCR 4.03(b); RAP 3(d)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing access: training and registration; bar number or pro hac vice notation on every filing","d":"E-filing is mandatory in all actions in circuit and family courts of “Active Counties”, and those courts may not offer lawyers fax or other alternatives except in an emergency. Users must complete Supreme Court-required training and register before getting credentials. Every e-filed document must carry an /s/ signature, address and phone number, and either the lawyer’s West Virginia State Bar ID or a notation that the lawyer is admitted pro hac vice.\nIn the Intermediate Court and the Supreme Court, e-filing is mandatory for lawyers in all actions; a lawyer unable to e-file needs leave by paper motion showing good cause. If the system is down, a missed deadline is cured by filing with a sworn declaration by noon on the clerk’s next business day.","c":"TCR 15A.04, 15A.06, 15A.11, 15A.22; RAP 38A(c), (e), (j), (s)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: West Virginia Rules of Professional Conduct (effective January 1, 2015)","d":"The Supreme Court of Appeals approved comprehensive amendments on September 29, 2014, effective January 1, 2015; the rules follow the ABA Model Rules’ numbering and much of their text. The Court’s rules page shows no later amendment history. In February 2026 the Court entered an order refusing to adopt proposed changes to the Rules; that order was not reviewed. Several later ABA changes are absent, including the 2018 advertising overhaul and Model Rule 8.4(g).","c":"W. Va. RPC (approval order Sept. 29, 2014); Recent Rules Orders list (Feb. 2026 refusal order, No. 25-602)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: written scope-and-fee statement required; no fee-division rule in the text","d":"Rule 1.5(b) requires that the scope of the representation and the basis or rate of the fee and expenses be communicated in writing, and that changes also be in writing. The ABA rule says only “preferably in writing”. Contingent-fee agreements must be signed by the client and must clearly notify the client of expenses owed whether or not the client prevails.\nThe West Virginia rule has no counterpart to ABA Model Rule 1.5(e), which conditions fee division between firms on proportionality or joint responsibility. Comment [5] says a fee may be divided on referral if the client agrees that the case may be referred.","c":"W. Va. RPC 1.5(b)–(d) & cmt. [5]","v":true},{"t":"No screening to cure a lateral lawyer’s conflict","d":"Rule 1.10(a) imputes a lawyer’s Rule 1.7 or 1.9 conflict to the whole firm unless the conflict rests on a personal interest that poses no significant risk to the firm’s representation. It omits the ABA Model Rule 1.10(a)(2) exception that lets a firm screen a laterally hired lawyer, so a lateral hire’s former-client conflict disqualifies the firm unless the client waives under Rule 1.7.","c":"W. Va. RPC 1.10(a), (c)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.2(e): advising on West Virginia law that conflicts with federal law","d":"A lawyer may counsel and assist a client on conduct the lawyer reasonably believes West Virginia law authorizes. Where West Virginia law conflicts with federal law, the lawyer must also advise on the related federal law and its potential consequences. The ABA Model Rules have no such paragraph.","c":"W. Va. RPC 1.2(e)","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: ethics advice from Disciplinary Counsel is a permitted disclosure","d":"Rule 1.6(b)(4) allows disclosure to obtain legal advice, or informal ethics advice from the Office of Disciplinary Counsel, about the lawyer’s own compliance. Otherwise the exceptions track the ABA rule, including the crime-fraud exceptions in (b)(2)–(3) and the conflict-check exception in (b)(7). There is no mandatory disclosure duty in Rule 1.6.","c":"W. Va. RPC 1.6(b)","v":true},{"t":"Advertising and specialization: pre-2018 rules remain","d":"Targeted written, recorded or electronic solicitations must be labeled “Advertising Material” on the envelope and at the beginning and end of any recorded or electronic message. Every advertisement must include the name and office address of at least one responsible lawyer or firm. Rule 7.4(d) states that West Virginia does not recognize specialization, so a lawyer may not state or imply certification as a specialist. Only “Patent Attorney” and admiralty designations are expressly allowed.","c":"W. Va. RPC 7.2(c), 7.3(c), 7.4(b)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"Other West Virginia variations","d":"- Rule 5.8 lets lawyers practice through LLCs or registered LLPs only if the firm registers with the West Virginia State Bar, and it keeps each lawyer personally liable for the lawyer’s own professional acts.\n- Rule 8.4 has no ABA 8.4(g) harassment and discrimination provision. TCR 4.06 separately bars biased conduct and statements toward others in court proceedings when unrelated to good-faith advocacy.\n- Rule 6.1 sets no numerical pro bono target.\n- Trust accounts must comply with State Bar Administrative Rule 10 on overdraft reporting and IOLTA (Rule 1.15(a), (f)).","c":"W. Va. RPC 1.15(a), (f), 5.8, 6.1, 8.4; TCR 4.06","v":true}],"style":"State of West Virginia","home":"https://www.courtswv.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"The notice of appeal is filed with the appellate Clerk, not the circuit clerk, within 30 days","d":"Appeals from final civil judgments entered after June 30, 2022 go to the Intermediate Court of Appeals. File the Appendix A notice of appeal, with all required attachments including the order appealed, in the Office of the Clerk of the Supreme Court/Intermediate Court within 30 days of entry. Serve it on all parties, on the circuit clerk, and on each court reporter from whom a transcript is ordered. The appellate court may extend the notice deadline for good cause, but the 3-day mail rule never extends it.","c":"RAP 5(b), 5(i)(1), 39(c); W. Va. Code § 51-11-4(b)(1)"},{"t":"The appeal is “perfected” by the opening brief and appendix within four months, with at most two months’ extension","d":"Unless law provides otherwise, the petitioner must file the brief and the appendix record within four months of entry of the judgment. Total extensions may not exceed two months, and failure to perfect results in dismissal. There is no automatic transmission of the record; the petitioner builds and files the appendix.","c":"RAP 5(f)–(g), 5(i)(2), 6(c), 7, 10(b)"},{"t":"No interlocutory appeals to the Intermediate Court","d":"The Intermediate Court has no jurisdiction over interlocutory appeals, certified questions or extraordinary writs. A non-final order is reviewed by petition for a writ of prohibition in the Supreme Court, which is discretionary and “sparingly exercised”, or by a certified question under § 58-5-2. A partial judgment is appealable only with an express Rule 54(b) no-just-reason-for-delay determination. The notice of appeal form makes you explain finality.","c":"W. Va. Code §§ 51-11-4(d)(8)–(10), 58-5-1(a), 58-5-2; RAP 1(b), 16(a); W. Va. R. Civ. P. 54(b)"},{"t":"After a jury trial, skipping a new-trial motion waives trial errors","d":"A party that does not file a timely Rule 59 new-trial motion after a jury verdict is deemed to have waived all trial errors it could have raised in that motion. A denied Rule 50(b) motion is the only exception. The motion is due 28 days after entry of judgment and cannot be extended.","c":"W. Va. R. Civ. P. 59(b), 59(f), 6(b)(2)"},{"t":"Local counsel must have a West Virginia office and must show up, including at depositions","d":"Pro hac vice requires association with a West Virginia lawyer whose primary daily office is physically in West Virginia. That lawyer must sign every served paper (unsigned papers are invalid) and attend every hearing, trial and deposition unless the judge permits remote attendance. Budget $350 per lawyer per case plus a $350 annual fee, and a fresh application for any appeal.","c":"W. Va. R. Admission 8.0(b), (c), (e), (f); RAP 3(c)"},{"t":"Judge-disqualification motions have short windows and a verified certificate","d":"There is no peremptory strike of a judge. A disqualification motion must be filed within 30 days of learning the ground, at least 7 days before a hearing or 21 days before trial, cite the specific Rule 2.11 ground, and carry a verified certificate of counsel. A late motion can be decided by the challenged judge, and rulings are not immediately appealable.","c":"TCR 17.01, 17.05, 17.07"},{"t":"Answers are due in 30 days, but motion responses in 21 days and hearing notices 14 days ahead","d":"Defendants answer in 30 days after service (and must attach a civil case information statement). By contrast:\n- motion response memoranda are due 21 days after service of the motion;\n- replies are due 7 days after the response;\n- a motion and notice of hearing must be served at least 14 days before the hearing;\n- supporting memoranda are capped at 20 pages without leave;\n- no pleading may be filed less than 48 hours before oral presentation or argument without permission.","c":"W. Va. R. Civ. P. 6(d), 12(a)(1); TCR 6.01(c), 22.01"},{"t":"Do not count on three extra days after e-service","d":"RCP 6(e) adds three days only after service by mail, by leaving the paper with the clerk, or by other consented means; e-service under the Trial Court Rules (RCP 5(b)(2)(E)) is not listed. TCR 15A.18 says e-service is “treated the same as service by mail” for Rules of Civil Procedure purposes. Until that conflict is resolved, calendar from the e-service date. On appeal, the 3-day mail addition never applies to docketing or perfecting deadlines or to court-ordered dates.","c":"W. Va. R. Civ. P. 5(b)(2)(E), 6(e); TCR 15A.18; RAP 39(c)"},{"t":"Six-person civil juries, two peremptories per side, and a 14-day jury demand","d":"A civil jury has six members unless the court orders more. Each side gets two peremptory challenges, exercised alternately starting with the plaintiff, and multiple parties on a side may be treated as one. A jury demand must be served within 14 days after the last pleading on the issue and filed, or the jury is waived.","c":"W. Va. R. Civ. P. 38(b), (d), 47(b), 48"},{"t":"Summary judgment motions are cut off 30 days after discovery closes","d":"Unless the court sets a different time, a summary-judgment motion may be filed only until 30 days after the close of all discovery. The court must state findings of fact and conclusions of law when it grants the motion, a common basis for reversal.","c":"W. Va. R. Civ. P. 56(b), 52(a)(3)"},{"t":"Election days are legal holidays, and the trial and appellate holiday lists differ","d":"Under RCP 6, any day on which a general, special or primary election is held statewide or in the county is a legal holiday, as are Lincoln’s Birthday and West Virginia Day. The appellate holiday list in RAP 39 adds Juneteenth, which RCP 6 does not list unless declared by the Governor or President. RAP 39 also excludes weekends and holidays from periods under seven days, while RCP 6 counts every day.","c":"W. Va. R. Civ. P. 6(a)(1), (a)(6); RAP 39(a)"},{"t":"Cases idle for a year can be dismissed","d":"A circuit court may dismiss any action pending more than one year in which the plaintiff has taken no action, after notice and an opportunity to be heard. Reinstatement requires good cause on a motion filed within three terms of court after the dismissal order. Actions may also be dismissed for unpaid accrued court costs.","c":"W. Va. R. Civ. P. 41(b)(2)–(4); TCR 2"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory substitution of a circuit judge; disqualification motions go to the Chief Justice","d":"No rule or statute collected gives a peremptory challenge to a judge. Under TCR 17.01 a party may move to disqualify only where the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned under Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11. Timing: the motion must be filed within 30 days after discovering the ground, and at least 7 days before any hearing or 21 days before any trial, final hearing or disposition date.\nThe motion must cite the specific provision of Rule 2.11 and include a verified certificate of counsel that it is well grounded, supported by sufficient evidence, and not filed for an improper purpose. A copy goes directly to the judge. On receiving it, the judge must proceed no further and send the motion to the Chief Justice with a letter responding to it. The Chief Justice must rule within 14 days and may order a hearing.\nA late motion may be decided by the judge, and if trial is within 21 days the judge need not send it up; the issue is then left for appeal. Improper motions can draw sanctions, including fees (TCR 17.07).","c":"TCR 17.01, 17.07","v":true},{"t":"Voluntary recusal, single-judge circuits, and review","d":"A judge who recuses on the judge’s own motion enters an order. In a multi-judge circuit the case is reassigned by local practice. Under the February 17, 2026 amendments, in a single-judge circuit the order asks the Chief Judge of the Raleigh County Circuit Court to assign one of its judges. Venue does not change and filings stay in the original county. A Chief Justice-appointed special judge who later cannot serve must report to the Chief Justice.\nAll disqualification rulings are interlocutory and not immediately appealable, though a writ of prohibition or mandamus remains available.","c":"TCR 17.02, 17.03, 17.05, 17.06 (as amended Feb. 17, 2026, No. 25-742)","v":true},{"t":"Statutory disqualification by relationship or interest","d":"W. Va. Code § 51-2-8 bars a circuit or intermediate judge from hearing a case without all parties’ written consent if the judge:\n- is a party or interested in the result;\n- is related to a party in listed degrees, up to first cousin;\n- or a listed relative is a stockholder or officer of a corporate party;\n- is a material witness.\nA judgment is not invalidated for the relationship unless it appears of record. A disqualified judge may still enter purely formal orders that advance the case.","c":"W. Va. Code § 51-2-8","v":true},{"t":"Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11 amended August 25, 2026","d":"The Court approved amendments to the Code of Judicial Conduct on August 25, 2026 (No. 25-882). The order’s Rule 2.11 lists the familiar Model Code grounds and adds “judge-designate” to the public-statement ground. It omits the Model Code’s campaign-contribution ground (A)(4), moves the duty to disclose possibly disqualifying information into the rule as 2.11(D), and allows waiver of non-bias grounds through agreement made outside the judge’s presence. The Clerk’s note says disqualification decisions remain subject to Chief Justice review under TCR 17. Because the extracted text cannot show which words were struck or added, and no effective date was found, check the current Code before quoting it.","c":"W. Va. Code of Judicial Conduct R. 2.11 (Order No. 25-882, Aug. 25, 2026)","v":false},{"t":"Appellate judges: Justices decide their own disqualification motions","d":"In the Supreme Court a motion to disqualify a Justice must be filed within 30 days after discovering the ground and at least 7 days before any scheduled proceeding, with a verified certificate. The challenged Justice decides it, and the Chief Justice may appoint a senior justice, senior judge or circuit judge as a substitute. Counsel must also tell the Clerk by letter, on appearing, of any circumstance that may create a disqualifying interest for a Justice. For an Intermediate Court judge, the motion goes to the Chief Justice, who rules within 14 days.","c":"RAP 33A(a)–(h), 33B(a)–(d)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Cameras in court are at the presiding judge’s discretion","d":"Cameras and audio equipment in and around courtrooms are allowed only at the discretion of the presiding circuit judge or magistrate. Requests must be made at least one day in advance, and any party, witness or counsel may object. The judge must end coverage that would impede justice or deny a fair trial. Coverage is limited to proceedings open to the public, and there may be no audio of attorney–client or bench conferences.","c":"TCR 8.01–8.04","v":true},{"t":"Sealing and limiting access to court files","d":"On motion by either party, a circuit court may limit access to a civil file by an order stating the nature, duration and reason for the limitation. The limit may be granted ex parte if the motion is filed with the complaint and supported by affidavit, and any person may later seek review (TCR 10.03).\nUnder RCP 5.2, confidential filings must be filed conventionally and conspicuously identified. A motion to seal must state the legal authority for confidentiality, the material stays confidential while the motion is pending, and any person with standing may move to unseal on good cause.","c":"TCR 10.03; W. Va. R. Civ. P. 5.2(a)","v":true}],"other":[{"t":"Business Court Division and Mass Litigation Panel","d":"Business litigation goes to the Business Court Division through a Motion to Refer filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court of Appeals, not the circuit clerk. Referred cases go to the Division, whose Chair assigns a presiding judge and a resolution judge. Qualifying cases involve commercial or technology disputes between business entities, and consumer, tort, employment, insurance bad-faith and similar cases are excluded. The State is divided into seven assignment regions. Mass torts may be referred to the Mass Litigation Panel under TCR 26, where TCR 15 e-filing applies.","c":"TCR 26, 29.04–29.06","v":true},{"t":"Separate case numbers and fees for each unrelated plaintiff","d":"A complaint joining individual plaintiffs who are not related by marriage or a derivative or fiduciary relationship must be docketed as a separate civil action for each plaintiff, with a separate fee. Each such action also needs its own pro hac vice admission and $350 fee.","c":"W. Va. R. Civ. P. 3(a); W. Va. R. Admission 8.0(c)","v":true},{"t":"Class-action residual funds: at least 25% to Legal Aid of West Virginia","d":"When class claims are exhausted and residual funds remain, at least 25% must go to Legal Aid of West Virginia. The court may distribute the remaining 75%, after notice and hearing, to West Virginia nonprofits, schools or foundations whose programs benefit the class, or to Legal Aid.","c":"W. Va. R. Civ. P. 23(h)","v":true},{"t":"Judgment interest rate for 2026: 6.25%","d":"The Supreme Court’s rules page states that, under W. Va. Code § 56-6-31 and an administrative order, the interest rate for judgments and decrees entered in 2026, including prejudgment interest, is 6.25%. The statute and the order were not collected.","c":"W. Va. Code § 56-6-31; Supreme Court of Appeals 2026 interest-rate order (as stated on courtswv.gov court-rules page)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"West Virginia Rules of Appellate Procedure as posted by the Supreme Court of Appeals (displayed November 10, 2026, reflecting 2026 amendments to Rules 5, 21 and 38A); Rules of Civil Procedure 50, 52, 54, 59, 60, 62, 77 (revised rules effective January 1, 2025); Trial Court Rule 17; W. Va. Code §§ 51-11-4, 51-11-5, 51-11-10, 58-5-1, 58-5-2, 58-5-4, 58-5-5, 58-5-14 (code.wvlegislature.gov, displayed November 10, 2026).","intro":{"t":"Appeals in West Virginia: a two-tier system since July 1, 2022","d":"Final circuit court judgments in civil cases entered after June 30, 2022 are appealed as of right to the Intermediate Court of Appeals. Review in the Supreme Court of Appeals then comes by a discretionary petition (styled a notice of appeal) filed within 30 days of the Intermediate Court’s mandate. The Supreme Court can pull a case up directly, either on its own motion or by granting a party’s motion for direct review filed within 20 days of the notice of appeal.\nThe statute leaves timing entirely to court rules (§ 58-5-4). The key steps are a notice of appeal filed with the appellate Clerk within 30 days of entry of the final order, and perfection of the appeal, meaning the petitioner’s brief plus an appendix the petitioner compiles, within four months of entry. Interlocutory review is not available in the Intermediate Court. It comes only through a writ of prohibition or a certified question in the Supreme Court.","c":"W. Va. Code §§ 51-11-4, 51-11-5, 51-11-10, 58-5-1, 58-5-4; RAP 1, 5"},"groups":[["Which court and what is appealable",[{"t":"Intermediate Court of Appeals: civil final judgments, family court, agencies, workers’ compensation","d":"The Intermediate Court hears final judgments or orders of circuit courts in all civil cases entered after June 30, 2022, as well as family court final orders (except domestic violence, which go first to circuit court), guardianship and conservatorship orders, agency and ALJ decisions, and Workers’ Compensation Board of Review decisions. It has no original jurisdiction. It cannot hear:\n- criminal, juvenile or abuse-and-neglect judgments;\n- commitment orders;\n- lawyer or judicial discipline;\n- Public Service Commission decisions;\n- interlocutory appeals;\n- certified questions;\n- extraordinary writs;\n- adoption decrees.","c":"W. Va. Code § 51-11-4(b), (d); RAP 1(b)"},{"t":"Final judgments and Rule 54(b) partial judgments","d":"A party may appeal a final judgment, or a judgment on fewer than all claims or parties if the circuit court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay and directs entry. Without that determination, an order resolving fewer than all claims remains revisable and is not final. The notice-of-appeal form asks whether the order is final as to all issues and parties or entered under Rule 54(b), and otherwise requires an explanation of appealability.","c":"W. Va. Code § 58-5-1(a); W. Va. R. Civ. P. 54(b); RAP App. A, item 9a"},{"t":"Direct review and Supreme Court takeover","d":"Within 20 days after a notice of appeal is filed in the Intermediate Court, a party may move in the Supreme Court for direct review. A response is due in 10 days, and replies need leave. The motion may be granted only if the appeal involves fundamental public importance and exigencies make time of the essence. If the Supreme Court grants it within 20 days after filing, jurisdiction transfers to the Supreme Court. The Supreme Court may also obtain jurisdiction over any civil case in the Intermediate Court on its own motion.","c":"W. Va. Code §§ 51-11-4(b)(1), 51-11-5; RAP 29(f)"}]],["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"Notice of appeal: 30 days from entry, filed with the appellate Clerk","d":"Within 30 days of entry of the final order (or the Intermediate Court’s mandate), the party appealing must file the Appendix A notice of appeal with all required attachments in the Office of the Clerk. Attachments include copies of all orders appealed and, for a Supreme Court appeal from the Intermediate Court, that court’s mandate. Serve the notice on all parties, on the circuit clerk (where it becomes part of the circuit record), and on each court reporter from whom a transcript is requested. The appellate court may extend the notice deadline for good cause on a Rule 39 motion. The Clerk charges $200 to docket a civil appeal in either court (in the Intermediate Court, family court and workers’ compensation appeals are excluded from that fee).","c":"RAP 5(b), 5(i)(1), 24(a); W. Va. R. Civ. P. 77(d)(2)"},{"t":"Parties, transcripts and the scheduling order","d":"All parties below are parties on appeal unless the notice of appeal designates them as having no interest. A party mistakenly so designated has 20 days from the notice to say otherwise.\nThe petitioner requests transcripts in the notice of appeal after getting estimates and making payment arrangements. A respondent wanting more transcripts must request them within 14 days after service of the notice. Court reporters have 45 days from receipt of the request to complete transcripts unless ordered otherwise.\nThe court then issues a scheduling order setting brief, appendix, transcript and motion dates; missing those dates can lead to sanctions or dismissal.","c":"RAP 5(c)–(e), 9(b)–(f)"},{"t":"Perfecting the appeal: four months from entry of judgment","d":"Unless law provides otherwise, an appeal must be perfected within four months of entry of the judgment by filing the petitioner’s brief (Rule 10) and the appendix record (Rule 7). Failure to perfect results in dismissal. The circuit court or the appellate court may extend the time for good cause, but by no more than two months in total, and only if a complete notice of appeal was timely filed. Leave to perfect without a notice of appeal requires a motion filed by the perfection deadline and extraordinary circumstances.","c":"RAP 5(f), 5(g), 5(i)(2)"}]],["Post-judgment motions and timing",[{"t":"Rule 50, 52 and 59 motions: 28 days, not extendable","d":"A renewed JMOL motion, a motion to amend findings, a new-trial motion and a motion to alter or amend are each due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, and the court may not extend those times. After a jury trial, failing to move for a new trial waives trial errors that could have been raised, except that a denied Rule 50(b) motion is preserved. Rule 60(b) motions must be made within a reasonable time, and within one year for mistake, newly discovered evidence or fraud; they do not affect finality.","c":"W. Va. R. Civ. P. 6(b)(2), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), (f), 60(c)"},{"t":"Tolling: not addressed in the collected rules","d":"Neither the Rules of Appellate Procedure nor the Rules of Civil Procedure as collected say whether a timely Rule 50(b), 52(b) or 59 motion postpones the 30-day notice-of-appeal period. No counterpart to Federal Appellate Rule 4(a)(4) was found. Any tolling rule would come from case law, which was not collected.\nConservative practice is to treat the order disposing of the motion as the operative final order but to file a protective notice of appeal, or seek a Rule 5(i)(1) extension, if there is any doubt. Remember that perfection runs four months from entry of the judgment being appealed.","c":"RAP 5(b), 5(f), 5(i); W. Va. R. Civ. P. 59"},{"t":"Lack of notice of entry does not extend the time","d":"The clerk must serve notice of entry of every order, but lack of notice does not affect the time to appeal except as RAP 5 allows. In e-filing counties the date of the e-filing court stamp is the date of entry.","c":"W. Va. R. Civ. P. 77(d); TCR 15A.25"}]],["Cross-appeals",[{"t":"Cross-assignments of error in the response brief; no separate notice needed","d":"A respondent who believes the record shows error to its prejudice may raise cross-assignments of error in a separate part of its brief, even without perfecting a separate appeal within the statutory period. The cover page must say so. The petitioner may respond in the reply brief, which then gets a 40-page limit and an automatic deadline of 30 days after the response. The respondent may file a non-duplicative supplemental appendix.","c":"RAP 10(f)–(g), 7(g)"}]],["Record, briefs and limits",[{"t":"The petitioner builds the appendix","d":"The record is not transmitted automatically. The petitioner must prepare an appendix containing:\n- the order appealed and related orders;\n- material pleadings and transcript excerpts;\n- critical exhibits;\n- the complete docket sheet.\nIt needs a cover page, a certification of accuracy and good-faith conferral, a table of contents, and sequential page numbers. Absent agreement, the petitioner serves a list of intended contents (plus any issues not in the notice of appeal), and the respondent has 10 days to add items. A designated record transmitted by the circuit clerk is allowed only by order. Sanctions motions for padding the appendix must be filed within 14 days after the decision and require a written objection within 10 days after the list was received.","c":"RAP 6(c)–(f), 7(c)–(e), 8"},{"t":"Briefing schedule","d":"Briefing follows the scheduling order. Typically the petitioner’s brief is due four months from entry of the final order, the respondent’s brief 45 days later, and the reply 20 days after that. Briefs are filed when received, not when mailed.\nInstead of a brief, a respondent may file a summary response of up to 15 pages, which waives oral argument. A respondent that fails to answer an assignment of error is deemed to agree with the petitioner on that issue.\nArguments must cite the record to show when and how each issue was preserved, and the court may disregard unsupported errors.","c":"RAP 10(b), 10(c)(7), 10(d)–(e)"},{"t":"Page limits and format","d":"Limits under Rule 38(c): 40 pages for petitioner’s and respondent’s briefs, 20 for a reply (30 with multiple response briefs, 40 with cross-assignments), 15 for a summary response, 15 for motions and responses, 15 for a rehearing petition, and 25 for an amicus brief. Covers, tables and certificates of service do not count.\nText must be double-spaced, at least 12-point proportional (or 11-point monospaced), with one-inch margins. West Virginia decisions must be cited with full parallel citations. Nothing may be filed less than 48 hours before argument. The Clerk may refuse non-compliant filings.","c":"RAP 10(g), 38(a)–(f)"},{"t":"Oral argument and decision","d":"Oral argument is unnecessary when waived, frivolous, authoritatively decided, or adequately briefed. Rule 19 arguments (settled law, discretion, sufficiency) get 10 minutes per side, and Rule 20 arguments (first impression, public importance, constitutional questions, conflicts) get 20 minutes. Notice issues at least 30 days ahead, and continuance motions are due within 10 days of the notice.\nCases may be decided by memorandum decision, which may be cited if labeled. Since June 25, 2026, a memorandum affirmance affirms the outcome but not necessarily the reasoning. After a Rule 20 argument, a memorandum decision requires exceptional or compelling circumstances.","c":"RAP 18–21 (Rule 21(c) as amended June 25, 2026, No. 25-846)"}]],["Rehearing, mandate and Supreme Court review",[{"t":"Rehearing within 30 days; mandate after 30 days","d":"A rehearing petition may be filed within 30 days of release of a merits opinion or memorandum decision. It is limited to 15 pages, must state points overlooked or misapprehended, and is granted only in exceptional cases. An optional response is due within 14 days. A timely petition stays the mandate. Otherwise the mandate issues soon after 30 days from release, or within 7 days after rehearing is denied. Decisions are not final until the mandate issues.","c":"RAP 25, 26(a)–(b), 21(f), 22(a)"},{"t":"Supreme Court review of the Intermediate Court: notice within 30 days of the mandate","d":"A party in interest may petition the Supreme Court for review of an Intermediate Court final order or judgment, and review is discretionary. The procedure is Rule 5: a notice of appeal filed within 30 days of the Intermediate Court’s mandate with the mandate attached, then perfection and briefing under the scheduling order. Filing the notice may support a stay of the Intermediate Court’s judgment under court rules. No appendix or designated record may contain material not before the Intermediate Court unless the Supreme Court grants leave.","c":"W. Va. Code § 51-11-10; RAP 5(a)–(b), 6(b)"},{"t":"Stay of mandate pending U.S. Supreme Court certiorari","d":"On motion, the Supreme Court may stay its mandate for up to 90 days pending a certiorari petition. The stay continues if the petition is filed and may be conditioned on security.","c":"RAP 26(c)"}]],["Interlocutory review, writs and certified questions",[{"t":"Writ of prohibition (and mandamus) in the Supreme Court","d":"Original-jurisdiction writs are discretionary and “sparingly exercised”. The petition, captioned “State ex rel.”, names the presiding judge and the real parties in interest as respondents and must be verified under W. Va. Code § 53-1-3. The argument must explain why relief is unavailable elsewhere. A rule to show cause in prohibition stays the underlying case. Denial of a rule to show cause is without prejudice unless stated otherwise and does not bar raising the issue on appeal after final judgment. Petitions are limited to 40 pages.","c":"RAP 16(a)–(j), 38(c)"},{"t":"Certified questions from circuit courts","d":"A circuit court may certify any question of law to the Supreme Court and stay the case. Examples include the sufficiency of service or a pleading, venue, personal or subject-matter jurisdiction, a denied summary-judgment motion, judgment on the pleadings, and joinder of an indispensable party. The certification order must state each question, the circuit court’s answer, the extent of the stay, and a direction to prepare a joint appendix. Federal and other courts certify under W. Va. Code § 51-1A-1 et seq. An order declining a certified question is not subject to rehearing.","c":"W. Va. Code § 58-5-2; RAP 17"},{"t":"Recusal rulings are interlocutory","d":"Trial-court recusal and disqualification rulings are not directly or immediately appealable; extraordinary writs remain available. The same rule applies to Intermediate Court judges.","c":"TCR 17.05; RAP 33B"}]],["Stays and bonds",[{"t":"Stay pending appeal starts in the circuit court","d":"Execution is automatically stayed for 30 days after entry of judgment, and a supersedeas stay may be obtained by court-approved bond. An application for a stay pending appeal goes first to the circuit court on written notice. If refused or inadequate, a motion may be made to the appellate court showing the lower court’s reasons, with a response due in 10 days.\nThe bond may not exceed the total judgment (including costs, interest and fees) and is capped at $50 million, adjusted for inflation every five years. The cap does not apply if the appellee proves the appellant is dissipating assets. Failure to post a required bond may be grounds for dismissal.","c":"W. Va. R. Civ. P. 62(a), (b), (e); RAP 28(a)–(c); W. Va. Code §§ 58-5-5, 58-5-14"}]],["E-filing and time computation",[{"t":"Mandatory e-filing in both appellate courts","d":"E-filing is mandatory for all actions in the Intermediate Court and the Supreme Court. A lawyer unable to e-file must move on paper for leave, showing good cause. A filing is complete only when the e-filing receipt is generated. E-service through the system is complete on the Notice of Electronic Filing. If the system is unavailable, a missed filing may be submitted with a sworn declaration by noon on the clerk’s next business day. Sealed documents are filed physically in sealed envelopes with an e-filed motion.","c":"RAP 38A(c), (o), (q), (s), (t) (as amended June 26, 2026, No. 25-890)"},{"t":"Computing appellate time","d":"Exclude the trigger day and include the last day, rolling past weekends and holidays. Periods under seven days exclude intermediate weekends and holidays. Three days are added after service by mail, but not to docketing or perfection deadlines or court-set dates. Courts may enlarge time for good cause, and a motion to hold a case in abeyance does not toll any deadline until granted.","c":"RAP 39(a)–(c), 28(f)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (circuit court final order to Intermediate Court)","d":"30 days from entry of the final order.\nFiled with the appellate Clerk; extendable for good cause (RAP 5(i)(1)); no 3-day mail extension (RAP 39(c)).","c":"RAP 5(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the final order)","note":"Filed with the appellate Clerk; extendable for good cause (RAP 5(i)(1)); no 3-day mail extension (RAP 39(c))."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal to the Supreme Court from the Intermediate Court","d":"30 days from entry of the Intermediate Court’s mandate.\nAttach the mandate; review is discretionary.","c":"RAP 5(b); W. Va. Code § 51-11-10","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the Intermediate Court’s mandate)","note":"Attach the mandate; review is discretionary."}},{"t":"Motion for direct review in the Supreme Court","d":"20 days from filing of the notice of appeal in the Intermediate Court.\nResponse due 10 days after filing.","c":"RAP 29(f); W. Va. Code § 51-11-5(a)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal in the Intermediate Court)","note":"Response due 10 days after filing."}},{"t":"Perfect the appeal (petitioner’s brief and appendix)","d":"120 days from entry of the judgment appealed.\nRule states four months, not 120 days; total extensions capped at two months (RAP 5(i)(2)).","c":"RAP 5(f), 10(b)","due":{"n":120,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment appealed)","note":"Rule states four months, not 120 days; total extensions capped at two months (RAP 5(i)(2))."}},{"t":"Respondent’s brief","d":"45 days from filing of the petitioner’s brief.\nTypical schedule; the scheduling order controls.","c":"RAP 10(b)","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the petitioner’s brief)","note":"Typical schedule; the scheduling order controls."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"20 days from filing of the respondent’s brief.\n30 days if the response contains cross-assignments of error.","c":"RAP 10(b), 10(g)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the respondent’s brief)","note":"30 days if the response contains cross-assignments of error."}},{"t":"Respondent’s additional transcript request","d":"14 days from service of the notice of appeal.","c":"RAP 9(d)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Party designated as uninterested asks to remain a party","d":"20 days from filing of the notice of appeal.","c":"RAP 5(c)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Respondent’s additions to appendix list","d":"10 days from receipt of the petitioner’s list.","c":"RAP 7(e)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (receipt of the petitioner’s list)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion for disposition by memorandum decision","d":"20 days from perfection of the appeal.\nResponse due 10 days after filing.","c":"RAP 21(b)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (perfection of the appeal)","note":"Response due 10 days after filing."}},{"t":"Response to a motion","d":"10 days from filing of the motion.\nReplies need leave; expedited-relief responses are due in 2 days (RAP 29(c)).","c":"RAP 29(a)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the motion)","note":"Replies need leave; expedited-relief responses are due in 2 days (RAP 29(c))."}},{"t":"Continuance or waiver of oral argument","d":"10 days from date of the notice of argument.","c":"RAP 19(c), (f); 20(c), (f)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (date of the notice of argument)","note":""}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing","d":"30 days from release of the opinion or memorandum decision.\nResponse optional, due 14 days after the petition.","c":"RAP 25(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (release of the opinion or memorandum decision)","note":"Response optional, due 14 days after the petition."}},{"t":"Mandate issues","d":"30 days from release of the decision (if no rehearing petition).\nWithin 7 days after denial of rehearing.","c":"RAP 26(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (release of the decision)","note":"Within 7 days after denial of rehearing."}},{"t":"Renewed JMOL / new trial / alter or amend / amend findings","d":"28 days from entry of judgment.\nNot extendable (Rule 6(b)(2)); effect on appeal time not stated in the collected rules.","c":"W. Va. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Not extendable (Rule 6(b)(2)); effect on appeal time not stated in the collected rules."}},{"t":"Family court appeal to the Intermediate Court: notice / perfection","d":"30 days from entry of the family court order.\nPerfect within 60 days; respondent 15 days and reply 10 days after; direct Supreme Court appeal requires both parties’ notice within 14 days (RAP 13(b)).","c":"RAP 13(c)(1), (3)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the family court order)","note":"Perfect within 60 days; respondent 15 days and reply 10 days after; direct Supreme Court appeal requires both parties’ notice within 14 days (RAP 13(b))."}},{"t":"Motion to disqualify a circuit judge","d":"30 days from discovery of the ground.\nAnd at least 7 days before a hearing or 21 days before trial.","c":"TCR 17.01(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (discovery of the ground)","note":"And at least 7 days before a hearing or 21 days before trial."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.courtswv.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"West Virginia Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"NOTE","t":"What this file covers","s":"These are the revised West Virginia Rules of Civil Procedure, which took effect January 1, 2025 and govern all civil actions in West Virginia trial courts of record except as stated in Rule 81 (the former rules remain posted for cases governed by them under Rule 86). The revision follows the restyled Federal Rules closely but keeps many West Virginia departures: a 30-day answer period, a broad subject-matter discovery scope, six-person civil juries with two peremptory challenges per side, a one-year inactivity dismissal, a 30-day automatic stay of execution, and a waiver rule for jury-trial errors not raised in a new-trial motion. Text is from the Supreme Court of Appeals’ rules page as displayed November 10, 2026; the Trial Court Rules (TCR) add statewide motion, e-filing, recusal and order-submission rules that are noted where they bear on a rule. Several cross-references in the posted text appear to be carried over from the federal model and do not match West Virginia numbering (for example Rule 15(c)(3) refers to “Rule 4(l)” and Rule 23(g) refers to “subdivision (h)”); those are flagged where they matter.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"1","t":"Scope and purpose","s":"The rules govern all civil actions and proceedings in West Virginia trial courts of record, except as stated in Rule 81, and are to be construed, administered and employed by the court and the parties to secure the just, speedy and inexpensive determination of every action.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the civil action.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencing an action","s":"An action is commenced by filing a complaint. Unlike federal practice, a complaint naming more than one individual plaintiff who are not related by marriage or a derivative or fiduciary relationship must be split: each plaintiff gets a separate civil action number, a separate docket and a separate filing fee. Every complaint must be accompanied by a completed civil case information statement (or its electronic equivalent) on the Supreme Court’s form.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"4","t":"Summons","s":"The clerk signs, seals and issues a summons for each defendant. Service may be made by any non-party aged 18 or older, by the sheriff, through the Secretary of State as statutory attorney-in-fact, or by the clerk by certified mail (restricted delivery, $20 per complaint) or first-class mail with a Form 11 notice and acknowledgment ($5 per complaint). Mail service cannot support a default unless the record shows a signed return receipt, a refused envelope, or a returned acknowledgment; if no acknowledgment comes back within 21 days of first-class mailing, another method must be used, and the court may shift the cost of personal service to a defendant who fails without good cause to return it. Rule 4(d) sets detailed methods for minors, incompetent and incarcerated persons, domestic and foreign corporations, public bodies and unincorporated associations; Rule 4(e) allows an order of publication (once a week for two successive weeks, answer date at least 30 days after first publication) on an affidavit of nonresidence, diligent search, or two unexecuted sheriff returns, and constructive service by certified mail with a 30-day answer period. Out-of-state personal service carries a 30-day answer period and counts as in-state personal service only for a West Virginia resident shown by affidavit. The time limit for service is 120 days after filing (federal: 90), with a mandatory extension for good cause.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"4.1","t":"Service of other process","s":"A rule, or an order in lieu of summons or rule, is served in the manner provided in Rule 4(d) unless the order says otherwise; Rule 45 governs subpoenas. All writs, process, returns, orders served or published and proofs of service are part of the record.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"5","t":"Serving and filing pleadings and other documents","s":"Pleadings after the complaint, discovery papers, written motions and notices must be served on every party; service on a represented party is made on the attorney. Service may be by hand, by leaving the paper at the office or dwelling, by mail (complete on mailing), by leaving it with the clerk if there is no known address, by e-service in e-filing counties under the Trial Court Rules or by fax under TCR 12, or by other electronic means only with written consent. Papers are filed with a certificate of service within a reasonable time, but Rule 26(a) disclosures and depositions, interrogatories, document requests and requests for admission are not filed until used or ordered (certificates of service for discovery are filed). The party who obtains discovery must keep custody of it until one year after final disposition.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"5.1","t":"[Reserved]","s":"Reserved; West Virginia has no counterpart to Federal Rule 5.1’s constitutional-challenge notice in this slot, but Rule 24(d) requires the court to notify the Attorney General when a state statute’s constitutionality is drawn in question in a case to which the State is not a party.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"5.2","t":"Privacy protection for filings","s":"Confidential filings must be filed conventionally (not electronically) and conspicuously identified; motions to seal or unseal must state the legal authority, the material stays confidential while a motion to seal is pending, and an opposing party has 10 days to respond. Filings must use initials in juvenile, sexual-offense-victim, abuse and neglect, mental hygiene and expungement cases; Social Security numbers may not be used (last four digits only), financial account numbers are limited to the last four digits, and birth dates and addresses may be used only when absolutely necessary. The court may enter a Medical Protective Order (Form 23) or a general confidentiality Protective Order (Form 24), and a party may file an unredacted copy or a reference list under seal.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"6","t":"Computing and extending time","s":"Day-based periods exclude the trigger day, count every day, and roll forward from a Saturday, Sunday or legal holiday; the last day ends at midnight for electronic or fax filing and at the clerk’s closing time otherwise. West Virginia legal holidays include Lincoln’s Birthday, West Virginia Day (June 20), Columbus Day, and any day on which a general, special or primary election is held statewide or in the county. All parties may change a period by written stipulation filed with the court; the court may extend for good cause, or after expiration for excusable neglect, but may not extend the times under Rules 50(b) and (d), 52(b), 59(b), (d) and (e), and 60(c). A written motion and notice of hearing must be served at least 14 days before the hearing; opposing affidavits are due 7 days before the hearing. Response memoranda are due within 21 days of service of the motion and replies within 7 days of service of the response; surreplies need leave. Three days are added after service by mail, by leaving with the clerk, or by other consented means (but not after e-service, which TCR 15A.18 nonetheless says is treated like mail service).","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions","s":"Only a complaint, answer, answer to a counterclaim, answer to a crossclaim, third-party complaint, third-party answer and, if ordered, a reply are allowed. A motion must be in writing unless made at a hearing or trial, state its grounds with particularity and state the relief sought. TCR 22.01 adds a 20-page limit on supporting memoranda absent leave and requires delivery of copies to the assigned judge.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"A claim requires a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for relief, and (unlike the federal rule) must be accompanied by a completed civil case information statement. Allegations other than the amount of damages are admitted if not denied in a required responsive pleading. The list of affirmative defenses still includes “discharge in bankruptcy”, which the federal list dropped in 2010, along with contributory negligence and injury by fellow servant; a mistaken designation of a defense as a counterclaim is corrected if justice requires.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity need not be pleaded but must be challenged by specific denial; fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity while malice, intent and knowledge may be alleged generally; conditions precedent may be pleaded generally but must be denied with particularity; special damages must be specifically stated. A West Virginia addition requires an eminent-domain pleading to describe the property and its intended use with particularity and, if the condemnor is a public utility, to attach its Certificate of Convenience and Necessity as a condition of maintaining the action.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Every pleading needs a caption with the court’s name, a title, the civil action number and a Rule 7(a) designation; claims and defenses go in numbered paragraphs; exhibits attached to a pleading are part of it for all purposes. TCR 6.01 sets statewide paper format: 8½ × 11, double-spaced, at least 12-point proportional type, one-inch margins, with the assigned judge’s name on the line below the case number and the filer’s bar number in the signature block.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing; representations to court; sanctions","s":"Every paper must be signed by an attorney of record (or an unrepresented party) and state the signer’s address, e-mail address, phone number and West Virginia State Bar ID number. Presenting a paper certifies the standard federal representations (proper purpose, warranted legal contentions, evidentiary support). A sanctions motion must be made separately and may not be filed if the challenged matter is withdrawn or corrected within 21 days after service (the safe harbor); the court may award the prevailing party reasonable expenses and fees, a law firm is jointly responsible absent exceptional circumstances, and no monetary sanction may be imposed on a represented party for a Rule 11(b)(2) violation. The rule does not apply to discovery under Rules 26–37.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections; when and how presented","s":"A defendant must answer within 30 days after service of the summons and complaint (federal: 21), and the answer must be accompanied by a civil case information statement; answers to counterclaims and crossclaims are also due in 30 days, and a court-ordered reply within 30 days. A Rule 12 motion suspends the deadline until 14 days after the court’s ruling or service of a more definite statement. The seven Rule 12(b) defenses, the waiver of personal-jurisdiction, venue, process and service defenses if omitted from a first Rule 12 motion or responsive pleading, and the preservation of failure-to-state-a-claim and Rule 19 defenses through trial track the federal rule. A motion to strike must be made before responding or within 21 days after service. TCR 20.01 allows the answer date to be extended by stipulation, which counts as an appearance, and TCR 22.02 lets a separately filed, prominently labeled motion to dismiss receive priority.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and crossclaim","s":"Counterclaims arising from the same transaction or occurrence are compulsory unless they were already the subject of another pending action or the opposing party sued by attachment without obtaining personal jurisdiction; others are permissive. The rules do not expand counterclaims or credits against the State. Crossclaims against co-parties must arise from the same transaction or occurrence or relate to property in the action. Subdivision (f) is marked abrogated.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may implead a nonparty who is or may be liable to it without leave if the third-party complaint is filed within 14 days after serving its original answer (or as a Rule 16(b) scheduling order provides); otherwise leave by motion is required. The third-party defendant asserts its Rule 12 defenses and Rule 13 claims, may raise the third-party plaintiff’s defenses against the plaintiff, and the plaintiff may assert related claims directly against it. A plaintiff facing a counterclaim may implead on the same terms. There is no admiralty provision.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as a matter of course within 21 days after serving the pleading or, if a response is required, within 21 days after service of the responsive pleading or a Rule 12(b), (e) or (f) motion, whichever is earlier; otherwise written consent or leave (freely given) is needed. A response to an amended pleading is due within the time left on the original or 14 days, whichever is later. Amendments relate back on the federal grounds; the posted text of Rule 15(c)(3) refers to service within “the period provided by Rule 4(l)”, which appears to mean the 120-day period in Rule 4(i). Amendments to conform to the evidence are allowed at any time, even after judgment.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial conference; scheduling; management","s":"Except in exempted case categories, the court must issue a scheduling order after the Rule 26(f) report or a scheduling conference, within the earlier of 120 days after any defendant is served or 90 days after any defendant appears, unless it finds good cause for delay. The order must limit the time to join parties, amend pleadings, complete discovery and file motions, and may address disclosures, expert reports, ESI and Rule 502 agreements. A schedule may be changed only for good cause and with the judge’s consent; an order after a final pretrial conference may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice. Sanctions for missing a conference, being unprepared or violating a pretrial order include the Rule 37(b) orders and mandatory fee-shifting unless the failure was substantially justified.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"17","t":"Plaintiff and defendant; capacity; public officers","s":"Actions are prosecuted in the name of the real party in interest; executors, administrators, guardians, bailees, express trustees and contracting parties may sue in their own names, and statutes may require suit in the name of the State or a subdivision for another’s use. Dismissal for a real-party defect is barred until a reasonable time for ratification, joinder or substitution. Capacity is determined by domicile for individuals, by the law of organization for corporations, and by West Virginia law for others. A guardian, committee, conservator or like fiduciary may sue or defend for a minor, incompetent or incarcerated person; otherwise a next friend or guardian ad litem acts.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims","s":"A party may join as many claims, legal or equitable, as it has against an opposing party, including contingent claims such as a money claim joined with a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"19","t":"Required joinder of parties","s":"A person subject to service must be joined if complete relief is impossible without the person or the person’s interest would be impaired or would expose a party to inconsistent obligations; if joinder would make venue improper and the joined party objects, that party is dismissed. When joinder is not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed, using the four federal factors. A claimant must plead the names of required persons not joined and why. The rule is subject to Rule 23. Unlike the federal rule, there is no reference to subject-matter jurisdiction being destroyed by joinder.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence or series and share a common question of law or fact; the court may order separate trials to prevent prejudice. Rule 3(a) still requires separate civil action numbers and fees for unrelated individual co-plaintiffs.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may add or drop a party on just terms at any time and may sever any claim.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"A plaintiff exposed to double or multiple liability may join the claimants as defendants and require them to interplead even if their claims lack a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability; a defendant may interplead by crossclaim or counterclaim. There is no statutory-interpleader counterpart.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"Certification requires the four Rule 23(a) prerequisites and one Rule 23(b) category, with the federal settlement-approval factors, objector rules and class-counsel standards. Three numbering points: the class-counsel provision is lettered (f) and the fee provision (g), although the text cross-refers to them as (g) and (h). The posted text contains no Rule 23(f)-style interlocutory appeal from certification orders. A West Virginia-specific subdivision (h) requires that at least 25% of residual class funds go to Legal Aid of West Virginia, with the remaining 75% distributable after notice and hearing to West Virginia nonprofits, schools or foundations whose programs benefit the class, or to Legal Aid.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Derivative actions","s":"A shareholder or member derivative complaint must be verified, allege contemporaneous ownership (or devolution by operation of law), and state with particularity the demand made on directors (and, if necessary, shareholders) or why none was made; settlement, dismissal or compromise needs court approval with notice ordered by the court. There is no federal-jurisdiction collusion allegation.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"23.2","t":"Actions relating to unincorporated associations","s":"Actions by or against association members as a class through named representatives may proceed only if the representatives will fairly and adequately protect the association’s and members’ interests; Rule 23(d) and (e) procedures apply.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right and permissive intervention follow the federal standards; the motion must be served under Rule 5, state the grounds and attach the proposed pleading. Under Rule 24(d), when the constitutionality of a West Virginia statute affecting the public interest is drawn in question in an action to which the State, its officers, agencies or employees are not parties, the court must notify the Attorney General.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"If a party dies and the claim survives, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days after service of a statement noting the death or the action by or against the decedent is dismissed; the motion and statement must be served on nonparties under Rule 4, in any judicial circuit. The court may continue an action by or against the representative of a party who becomes incompetent or incarcerated, and may substitute or join a transferee. A public officer’s successor is substituted automatically.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"26","t":"Duty to disclose; general provisions governing discovery","s":"Initial disclosures (witnesses, documents, damages computation, insurance) are due within 30 days after the Rule 26(f) written report is filed; parties joined later have 30 days. Exempt proceedings include eminent domain, Board of Equalization and Review appeals, administrative summons enforcement, ancillary proceedings, arbitration-award enforcement, administrative-record review, appellate review of inferior bodies, and any matter where the agreed amount in controversy is under $25,000. Expert disclosures with written reports for retained experts are due at least 90 days before trial (rebuttal within 30 days of the other side’s disclosure); pretrial disclosures are due 30 days before trial, with objections within 14 days or waived (except under Evidence Rules 402 and 403). The scope of discovery is broader than the federal rule: any non-privileged matter “relevant to the subject matter involved in the pending action”, with proportionality factors applied only as a court-imposed limit under Rule 26(b)(2)(C). Draft reports and attorney–expert communications are protected on the federal model. Protective-order motions need a meet-and-confer certification. Discovery may not begin before the 26(f) conference, but Rule 34 requests may be delivered more than 21 days after service. The parties must confer “as soon as practicable—and in any event at least 30 days after” a responsive pleading or Rule 12 motion is filed (the posted wording appears to mean within 30 days) and file a written discovery-plan report within 14 days after the conference. Expert supplementation is due 30 days before trial. Discovery signatures must include an e-mail address.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions to perpetuate testimony","s":"Before suit, a person expecting to be a party may file a verified petition in the circuit where any expected adverse party resides and must serve each expected adverse party under Rule 4 at least 21 days before the hearing; the court appoints counsel for those not personally served. Pending appeal, the trial court may permit perpetuation depositions on motion. A perpetuation deposition may be used in any later action on the same subject matter in a West Virginia court under Rule 32(a).","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"In the United States, before an officer authorized by federal law, West Virginia law, or the law of the place of examination, or a court-appointed person; abroad, under a treaty, letter of request, notice before an authorized officer, or commission. A deposition may not be taken before a party’s relative, employee or attorney, a person related to or employed by a party’s attorney, or anyone financially interested in the action.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations about discovery procedure","s":"Parties may stipulate to deposition logistics and to modify discovery procedures, but a stipulation extending discovery time needs court approval if it would interfere with the deadline for completing discovery, a motion hearing, or trial.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions by oral examination","s":"Leave is required to depose before the Rule 26(d) time or to depose an incarcerated person, except for a plaintiff’s notice certifying that the witness is about to leave the State (signed by the attorney as a certification). The text has no 10-deposition limit or federal leave requirement for a second deposition. The noticing party states the recording method and bears its cost; any deposition may be taken remotely, the deposition takes place where the deponent answers, and the noticing party must ensure (and for a non-party pay for) the witness’s technology. Organizational depositions require a meet-and-confer on topics. Objections must be concise and non-suggestive, and instructions not to answer are limited to privilege, court limits or a Rule 30(d)(3) motion. Duration is limited to one day of 7 hours. The deponent has 30 days after notice that the transcript is available to review and sign changes if review is requested before the deposition ends.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions by written questions","s":"Leave is required only for a deponent confined in prison. Cross-questions are due within 14 days after service of the notice and direct questions, redirect within 7 days after cross, and recross within 7 days after redirect; the court may change these times.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"32","t":"Using depositions in court proceedings","s":"A deposition may be used against a party who was present, represented or had reasonable notice, to the extent admissible. An adverse party may use a party’s or its officer’s or designee’s deposition for any purpose. Any party may use a witness’s deposition for any purpose if the witness is dead, “out of the state” (rather than the federal 100-mile test) unless the offering party procured the absence, unable to attend because of age, illness, infirmity or imprisonment, or not subject to subpoena, or in exceptional circumstances. A deposition taken on less than 14 days’ notice may not be used against a party whose prompt protective-order motion was still pending. Objection-waiver rules track the federal rule; objections to written questions’ form are due within the time for responsive questions or 7 days for recross.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Each party may serve up to 25 interrogatories including discrete subparts, unless stipulated or ordered. Answers and objections are due within 30 days after service, each answer under oath; unstated objections are waived absent good cause. Contention interrogatories are allowed but may be deferred. A party may answer by specifying business records when the burden of deriving the answer is the same for both sides.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"34","t":"Producing documents, ESI and things; entry on land","s":"A request must describe items with reasonable particularity and may specify the ESI form. The written response is due within 30 days after service, or within 30 days after the first Rule 26(f) conference for an early request delivered under Rule 26(d)(2). Objections must be stated with specificity and say whether responsive materials are being withheld; production must be completed by the time stated in the request or another reasonable time stated in the response. Documents are produced as kept or labeled by category; ESI in the form ordinarily maintained or a reasonably usable form, and only once. The text sets no numerical limit on requests.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"On motion for good cause and notice, the court may order a party whose physical or mental condition (including blood group) is in controversy to be examined by a suitably licensed or certified examiner, specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope and examiner. The examined party may obtain the detailed report and thereby waives privilege as to other examinations of the same condition; the moving party may then obtain reciprocal reports. A report not provided may result in exclusion of the examiner’s testimony.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Requests may cover facts, application of law to fact, opinions about either, and the genuineness of documents. A matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days after service. Lack of knowledge is a permissible answer only after reasonable inquiry; a request may not be objected to solely because it presents a genuine issue for trial. Admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on motion, and bind only in the pending action. No numerical limit is stated.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to make disclosures or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","s":"Motions to compel need a good-faith conferral certification; a motion directed to a nonparty is filed in the circuit court where the discovery is taken. Expense-shifting follows the federal model, but the posted text of Rule 37(a)(5)(A)(i) excuses payment if “the movant filed the motion good faith to obtain the disclosure” (the federal rule excuses payment when the movant filed before attempting in good faith to obtain it), which reads as a drafting error. Sanctions for violating an order range from deemed facts to dismissal, default and contempt, with mandatory expenses unless substantially justified. Undisclosed information or witnesses are excluded unless the failure was substantially justified or harmless. ESI-loss sanctions track the 2015 federal Rule 37(e): curative measures on prejudice, and adverse-inference instructions, dismissal or default only on a finding of intent to deprive. Failure to participate in good faith in framing the Rule 26(f) plan can result in fee-shifting.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"A jury demand must be served no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue and filed under Rule 5(d); another party may demand jury on other issues within 14 days after service of a partial demand. A party waives a jury unless its demand is properly served and filed, and a proper demand may be withdrawn only with the parties’ consent.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or the court finds no jury right exists; the court may order a jury on any issue even without a proper demand, and may use an advisory jury or, with consent, a binding jury in non-jury actions.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"40","t":"Scheduling cases for trial","s":"Trials may be set by the court on its own, by Rule 16 scheduling order, or on motion, with priority to actions entitled to it by constitution, statute or rule.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions or claims","s":"A plaintiff may dismiss without order by notice before an answer or summary-judgment motion is served, or by stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared; a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise dismissal requires a court order. Involuntary dismissals operate on the merits unless the order says otherwise or the dismissal is for lack of jurisdiction, venue or Rule 19 joinder. West Virginia additions: the court may dismiss any action pending more than one year in which the plaintiff has taken no action, and may reinstate it for good cause on a motion filed within three terms of court after the dismissal order (TCR 2 sets each circuit’s terms); it may dismiss for unpaid accrued court costs (reinstatement only after payment); and notice and an opportunity to be heard must precede any Rule 41(b) dismissal. Costs of a previously dismissed action may be ordered before a refiled action proceeds.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"The court may join for hearing or trial, or consolidate, actions sharing a common question, including appeals from magistrate court. When actions arising from the same transaction or occurrence are pending before different circuit judges, or a circuit judge and a magistrate court, the judge before whom the first action was commenced must order them transferred to one court. A magistrate-court judgment of $15.00 or less has no res judicata or evidentiary effect on a related circuit court action. Separate trials of issues or claims may be ordered, preserving jury rights.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"43","t":"Taking testimony","s":"Trial testimony is taken in open court unless a statute or rule provides otherwise; affirmation may replace an oath; motions may be heard on affidavits, oral testimony or depositions; the court may appoint and set compensation for an interpreter and tax it as costs. The text has no federal-style provision for remote trial testimony (see TCR 14 on videoconferencing).","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"44","t":"Proving an official record","s":"A domestic official record is proved by official publication or an attested copy with a custody certificate under seal from a judge of the circuit or subdivision or a public officer with a seal; foreign records by publication or an attested copy with final certification or treaty certification, or for good cause without it. A certified no-record statement proves absence of a record.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Determination of foreign law","s":"A party intending to raise foreign-country law must give notice by pleading or other writing; the court may consider any relevant source, and its determination is a ruling on a question of law.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"Subpoenas issue from the court where the action is pending, either from the clerk signed in blank or signed by an attorney authorized to practice in that court (so an out-of-state lawyer needs pro hac vice admission to sign one). Before serving a pre-trial document or inspection subpoena, notice and a copy must be served on each party. Service is made as for personal service of process under Rule 4(d)(1)(A), with one day’s fees and mileage tendered if demanded, and a subpoena may be served anywhere in West Virginia (there is no nationwide or 100-mile provision). A deponent can be required to attend only in the county of residence, employment or in-person business, or a place fixed by court order. Written objections to production are due before the earlier of the compliance date or 14 days after service.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"46","t":"Objecting to a ruling or order","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; a party need only state the action it wants or objects to and the grounds, and a party with no opportunity to object is not prejudiced.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"47","t":"Selecting jurors","s":"The court may let counsel conduct voir dire or conduct it itself while allowing supplemental questions. A civil jury consists of six persons unless the court directs a larger number, and the plaintiff and defendant each get two peremptory challenges, exercised one at a time alternately beginning with the plaintiff; multiple parties on a side may be treated as one or given additional challenges. Up to six alternates may be seated, with one extra peremptory per side for one to three alternates and two for four to six, usable only against alternates.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"48","t":"Juries; verdict; polling","s":"The parties may stipulate to fewer than six jurors. The verdict must be unanimous unless the parties stipulate otherwise, and the court must poll the jury on request.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"49","t":"Special verdicts; general verdict and questions","s":"Tracks the federal rule: the court may require special verdicts or a general verdict with written questions, and a party waives jury trial on an issue not submitted unless it demands submission before the jury retires. Inconsistent answers may lead to judgment on the answers, further deliberation, or a new trial.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"50","t":"Judgment as a matter of law in jury trials","s":"A JMOL motion may be made any time before submission to the jury and must specify the judgment sought and the law and facts supporting it. A renewed motion, with an optional alternative new-trial request, must be filed no later than 28 days after entry of judgment (or after discharge of the jury if no verdict decided the issue). If the renewed motion is granted, the court must rule conditionally on the new-trial motion. A new-trial motion by the party against whom JMOL is entered is due within 28 days of judgment. These times cannot be extended (Rule 6(b)(2)).","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"51","t":"Instructions to the jury; objections; preserving error","s":"Requested instructions are filed at the close of evidence or earlier when the court orders. The court must inform the parties in writing of its proposed instructions before instructing, give an opportunity to object on the record outside the jury’s hearing, and may send written instructions to the jury room. Error may be assigned only to properly objected-to instructions or properly requested and objected-to refusals; unpreserved plain error affecting substantial rights may be considered. TCR 23.02 governs the submission of proposed instructions and verdict forms.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings and conclusions by the court","s":"In bench trials the court must find facts specially and state conclusions separately; findings and conclusions are also required for preliminary injunctions and when the court grants a motion under Rule 12, 23(c)(1) or 56 (a requirement the federal rule does not impose). Findings are set aside only if clearly erroneous. A motion to amend findings is due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment and cannot be extended. Judgment on partial findings must be supported by findings and conclusions.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"53","t":"Commissioners; discovery commissioners","s":"Commissioners may be appointed under statute or rule. Discovery commissioners should be used only in complex cases; objections to an appointment order are due within 14 days of its entry, and compensation may be assessed to the parties. A discovery commissioner issues a non-binding written report and recommendation; objections are due within 7 days of service, responses within 5 days after service of objections, and replies within 5 days; untimely objections are waived, and discovery on disputed matters stops until the court acts. The court may adopt, reverse or modify by written order, set a hearing, or remand. This replaces the federal master rule.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments; costs","s":"A judgment includes any order from which an appeal lies. With multiple claims or parties, partial final judgment requires an express determination that there is no just reason for delay; otherwise the order is revisable (W. Va. Code § 58-5-1(a) ties appealability to this determination). Costs other than fees go to the prevailing party as of course; the clerk must tax costs within 14 days after judgment, and a motion for court review is due within 14 days after receipt of the bill of costs (federal: 7 days). Attorney-fee motions are due no later than 14 days after entry of judgment unless a statute or order says otherwise. The posted text says fee submissions are to be in accordance with “Rule 4(e) or 78”, an apparent cross-reference error carried from the federal rule (which refers to Rule 43(c) or 78).","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"55","t":"Default","s":"The clerk enters default on a showing by affidavit or otherwise that a party has failed to plead or defend. The clerk may enter a default judgment for a sum certain on affidavit against a defaulted defendant who is not a minor or incompetent person; otherwise application is made to the court, and a party that has appeared must get written notice at least 7 days before the hearing. Rule 4 bars a default based on clerk mail service unless the return receipt, refusal or acknowledgment is in the record. Default may be set aside for good cause and a default judgment under Rule 60(b).","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"Summary judgment is granted if there is “no genuine issue as to any material fact” and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. Unless the court sets a different time, a motion may be filed at any time until 30 days after the close of all discovery. Factual positions must cite particular record materials; the nonmovant may obtain deferral by affidavit showing it cannot yet present essential facts. When judgment is not rendered on the whole case, the court should, if practicable, enter an order specifying facts without substantial controversy, which are then deemed established at trial. Rule 52(a)(3) requires findings and conclusions when the court grants summary judgment. Response timing follows Rule 6(d)(3) (21 days after service of the motion).","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Declaratory judgment actions under the Uniform Declaratory Judgments Act, W. Va. Code § 55-13-1 et seq., proceed under these rules with jury rights under Rules 38 and 39. Another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief; declaratory and coercive relief may be sought together; further relief may be granted in the action or by petition; and the court may expedite.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry of judgments","s":"The court settles or approves the form of the judgment and signs it; the clerk’s notation in the civil docket is the entry, and the judgment is not effective before entry. Entry is not delayed for costs or post-trial motions. There is no separate-document requirement or 150-day deemed-entry rule as in the federal rule. In e-filing counties the date of the official e-filing court stamp is the date of entry (TCR 15A.25). TCR 24.01 requires counsel directed to prepare an order to submit it within 11 days, with 5 days for objections.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"59","t":"New trials; amendment of judgments","s":"A new trial may be granted after a jury trial for any reason previously allowed at law, or after a bench trial for any reason previously allowed in equity. Motions for a new trial and to alter or amend are due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, and cannot be extended; opposing affidavits are due 14 days after service. The court may order a new trial on its own within 28 days. A West Virginia addition, Rule 59(f): after a jury trial, a party who does not timely move for a new trial waives all trial errors that could have been raised in the motion, except that a denied Rule 50(b) motion is preserved.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time, but only with the appellate court’s leave once an appeal is docketed. Rule 60(b) grounds include “unavoidable cause” alongside mistake, inadvertence, surprise and excusable neglect. Motions must be made within a reasonable time, and for grounds (1)–(3) no more than one year after entry; the time cannot be extended. The motion does not suspend finality. Independent actions and relief for fraud on the court remain available, and bills of review and coram nobis are abolished.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors and defects that do not affect any party’s substantial rights are disregarded and are not grounds for a new trial or for disturbing a judgment.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"Execution is automatically stayed for 30 days after entry of judgment unless the court orders otherwise. A party may obtain a stay at any time by bond or other security, effective when the court approves it. The court may stay execution pending Rule 52(b), 59(e) or 60 motions, may stay a Rule 54(b) judgment, and on motion may stay execution for a reasonable time specified in the order to allow the moving party to present an appeal. Appeal-bond amounts are capped by W. Va. Code § 58-5-14 at the total judgment and at $50 million (CPI-adjusted) unless the appellant is dissipating assets, and stays pending appeal are governed by Rule 28 of the Rules of Appellate Procedure.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"63","t":"Judge’s inability to proceed","s":"A successor judge may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record and determining the case can be completed without prejudice; in a hearing or bench trial the successor must, on request, recall any available witness whose material testimony is disputed.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizure of person or property","s":"State-law prejudgment remedies (arrest, attachment, garnishment, seizure of specific property, sequestration) are available. Orders for seizure of specific personal property must be returned within 21 days after issuance; orders of civil arrest or attachment within 30 days; and a garnishee must answer within 90 days after service of the attachment order unless the answer is waived.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions and restraining orders","s":"Preliminary injunctions require notice and may be consolidated with trial. A TRO without notice requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint and counsel’s certification of notice efforts, expires within the time set (not over 14 days) unless extended for good cause for a like period or by consent, and the adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. Security is required in an amount the court considers proper, except from the United States, the State of West Virginia and its political subdivisions, officers and agencies. Findings and conclusions are required under Rule 52(a)(2).","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"65.1","t":"Security; proceedings against sureties","s":"A surety submits to the court’s jurisdiction and appoints the clerk as agent for service; liability may be enforced by motion without an independent action.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"An action in which a receiver has been appointed may not be dismissed without court order; appointment and administration follow West Virginia practice, and the rules otherwise govern.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit in court","s":"With notice and leave of court, a party may deposit money or a deliverable thing in dispute and must serve the order on the clerk; funds go into a federally insured interest-bearing account or a court-approved interest-bearing instrument.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment; payment into court","s":"A defending party may serve an offer of judgment more than 14 days before trial; the offeree has 14 days to accept in writing. If the judgment obtained is not more favorable than an unaccepted offer, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer. West Virginia keeps a payment-into-court procedure: a defending party may deposit a sum with the clerk and plead that no more is owed, and the claimant may accept with costs, reject, or accept as part payment and try damages only. After liability is determined, an offer may be made at least 14 days before the damages hearing.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"69","t":"Executions and other final process","s":"Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution, suggestee execution and other statutory writs under state procedure. A writ of execution is returnable not less than 30 nor more than 90 days after issuance; an answer to a suggestion summons is due within 21 days of service; a suggestee execution is returned after one year. A writ of possession must be returned within 21 days. Post-judgment discovery is available under these rules and by statute.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"If a party fails to perform a judgment for a specific act, the court may have it done by another at the party’s expense, divest and vest title to property within the State, issue writs of attachment, sequestration, execution or assistance, and hold the party in contempt.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"71","t":"Enforcing relief for or against a nonparty","s":"An order for or enforceable against a nonparty is enforced as if the nonparty were a party.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"71.1","t":"Eminent domain","s":"The rules govern circuit court eminent domain proceedings to the extent consistent with the constitution and statutes. The jury consists of twelve freeholders meeting W. Va. Code § 54-2-10. Rule 9(h) imposes special pleading requirements.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"71.2","t":"Extraordinary writs in circuit court","s":"The rules govern extraordinary writs in circuit court except as this rule provides. A plaintiff may join demands for different writs and other relief, names as defendants the state agencies, entities, subdivisions or individuals to whom relief is directed, and pleads the authority for the writ. A defendant may concede and the court then enters an appropriate writ; a contesting defendant answers. On default the court may not enter a Rule 55(b) default judgment but must hold a hearing on the relief demanded. Some rule sets (such as the Rules of Procedure for Administrative Appeals) still refer to the former numbering, Rule 71B.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"72","t":"Appeals","s":"Appeals are governed by the West Virginia Rules of Appellate Procedure.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"73","t":"[Abrogated]","s":"Abrogated; see the Rules of Appellate Procedure.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"74","t":"[Reserved]","s":"Reserved.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"75","t":"[Reserved]","s":"Reserved.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"76","t":"[Reserved]","s":"Reserved.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"77","t":"Court and proceedings; clerk’s authority; notice of orders; indigents","s":"Courts are always open for filing; trials on the merits are in open court, and no non-ex parte hearing may be held outside the circuit without all affected parties’ consent. The clerk may issue process, enter defaults and clerk default judgments. The clerk must serve notice of every order or judgment immediately after entry, but lack of notice does not extend the time to appeal except as Rule 5 of the Rules of Appellate Procedure allows. Rule 77(e) sets the fee-waiver procedure under W. Va. Code § 59-2-1: the clerk accepts the sworn financial affidavit as true and files the action if it meets the Supreme Court’s guidelines; on review of a denial the judge must act within 7 days; and filing the affidavit and application tolls any statute of limitations or other time requirement.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"78","t":"Motion day; hearings; submission on briefs","s":"A court may set regular motion days and provide for deciding motions on briefs without a hearing. TCR 22.03 allows telephone hearings with the judge’s permission and requires notice to all parties when a motion is decided without a hearing.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"79","t":"Records kept by the clerk","s":"The clerk keeps the civil docket, judgment and order records, indexes and trial calendars in the form the Supreme Court prescribes, notes “jury” for demanded cases, and may keep records digitally. In e-filing counties the electronic register of actions is the docket (TCR 15A.25).","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"80","t":"Transcript or statement of evidence made part of record","s":"A certified transcript becomes part of the record when filed, and the filing party must notify all other parties; any party may move to correct errors. When no transcript is available, a party may prepare a statement of the proceedings, which must be served within a reasonable time; other parties have 14 days to object or propose amendments, and the statement becomes part of the record when the judge approves, signs and files it. Bills and certificates of exception are abolished. (This is the trial-court counterpart to Federal Appellate Rule 10(c).)","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"81","t":"Applicability of the rules in general","s":"In circuit court appeals from magistrate court the rules apply, but Rules 26–37 may not be used and only magistrate-court pleadings are allowed absent an order. The rules apply in circuit court review of agency decisions when testimony is taken. In family court divorce and related cases they apply only as the Family Court Rules authorize; in circuit court divorce cases pleadings must be verified, no divorce is granted on uncorroborated testimony unless a statute allows it, and discovery requires a court order. Special rules apply to sales of forfeited and delinquent lands. In ex parte proceedings (adoption, name change, certain statutory summary procedures) and juvenile proceedings, only Rules 5(b), 5(d)(2), 5(d)(3) and 80 apply unless ordered. The rules may be applied to post-conviction habeas petitions in circuit court where consistent with the habeas rules.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","s":"The rules neither extend nor limit circuit court jurisdiction or venue.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"83","t":"Local rules","s":"Circuit courts may adopt local rules consistent with these rules and the Trial Court Rules, effective only after filing with and approval by the Supreme Court of Appeals. TCR 1.01–1.03 add that the Rules of Civil Procedure and Trial Court Rules preempt any differing local rule and no local procedure is effective unless adopted and approved as a local rule.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"84","t":"Forms","s":"The Appendix of Forms is sufficient under the rules (for example Form 11, notice and acknowledgment of mail service; Forms 23 and 24, medical and general protective orders). The forms were not separately collected.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"85","t":"Title","s":"These rules are the West Virginia Rules of Civil Procedure.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"86","t":"Effective date","s":"Rules and amendments take effect on the date set in the Supreme Court’s adopting order and govern later-filed cases and, as far as just and practicable, pending cases. The Court’s rules page states the revised rules govern on and after January 1, 2025.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"},{"n":"87","t":"[Abrogated]","s":"Abrogated.","u":"https://www.courtswv.gov/legal-community/court-rules/rules-civil-procedure-contents"}]});
